    {
      "id": "hostile/uint/negative",
      "type": "uint",
      "position": "method_arg",
      "method": "echoUint",
      "args": [
        -1
      ],
      "expect": {
        "__error__": "dispatch_failed"
      },
      "tags": [
        "hostile"
      ],
      "why": "both providers now REJECT a negative for a `uint`, so the divergence is CLOSED. The C++ side used to answer 18446744073709551615: its cdylib dispatch decoded scalars with a bare `.get<uint64_t>()`, which wraps -1 rather than throwing — a silent sign flip on a nominal value. Fixed by routing every typed scalar through the generated codec AND giving that codec a signedness check; the previous expectation was itself the stated reason the leniency was left in place, which made the argument circular."
    },
